    "gp_section_644851690129851030": {
      "settings": {
        "checksum": "863abd97a551247718efca86c52be5903fe2279a13d468bef9e1b2cd9dbba42c_1_1_1_0__1",
        "ggA6Aft0001_text": "<p><span style=\"background-color:#D9E6DC;color:#22332A;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:1.5px\"><strong>&nbsp;AFTER&nbsp;</strong></span></p><p><span style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4',serif;font-size:19px\"><strong>What the click trains, session after session</strong></span></p><p>✓ Holding lag through the bottom of the swing</p><p>✓ More compressed, consistent ball striking</p><p>✓ Straighter flight, tighter dispersion</p><p>✓ More solid strikes with the same effort</p>",
        "ggA6Bef0001_text": "<p><span style=\"background-color:#F0D9D3;color:#8A3B2A;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:1.5px\"><strong>&nbsp;BEFORE&nbsp;</strong></span></p><p><span style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4',serif;font-size:19px\"><strong>What early release costs you</strong></span></p><p>— Casting the club from the top of the downswing</p><p>— Weak impact, thin and fat contact</p><p>— Ball flight that drifts right (for right-handers)</p><p>— Lost carry with the driver</p>",
        "ggA6H20001_text": "<span style=\"color:#B5813A;font-size:18px;font-weight:400\">02</span> What it fixes, specifically",
        "ggA6OffB001_label": "<p>See today’s offer →</p>",
        "ggA6OffT001_text": "<p><span style=\"color:#B5813A\"><strong>25% off</strong></span> plus free shipping on $50+ orders.</p>",
        "ggA6TxA0001_text": "<p>Most amateur golfers fight one of two timing problems. They release too early (the dreaded casting motion), or they hang on too long and block the face open. Both produce the same outcome: weak contact, lost distance, and a ball that never quite goes where you aimed it.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>ARC ONE cuts through the vocabulary. You don’t need to memorize what <i>lag</i> feels like or study frame-by-frame video. You just swing until the click shows up at the ball. Then you swing again and make it show up in the same place.</p>",
        "ggA6TxB0001_text": "<p>It’s worth saying the quiet part out loud: this isn’t new physics. Coaches have taught lag and release timing for a hundred years. What’s new is feeling it happen in your own hands without paying for a studio session.</p>"
      },
      "type": "gp-section-644851690129851030"
    },
    "gp_section_644851724707693495": {
      "settings": {
        "checksum": "d057186b24315998c1d30ca5325140335260f8f0b1c3e31a168bbea52dfb354a_1_1_1_0__1",
        "ggA7H20001_text": "<span style=\"color:#B5813A;font-size:18px;font-weight:400\">03</span> Ten minutes a day, anywhere in the house",
        "ggA7StT0001_text": "<p><span style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4',serif;font-size:42px;color:#22332A\"><strong>10</strong></span></p><p>MINUTES A DAY</p>",
        "ggA7StT0002_text": "<p><span style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4',serif;font-size:42px;color:#22332A\"><strong>0</strong></span></p><p>BALLS, APPS OR BATTERIES</p>",
        "ggA7StT0003_text": "<p><span style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4',serif;font-size:42px;color:#22332A\"><strong>15.2\"</strong></span></p><p>FOLDED, FITS YOUR BAG</p>",
        "ggA7TxA0001_text": "<p>The routine is simple. Ten minutes a day. No balls, no net, no new clubs, no tweaks to your setup. Just the trainer, in whatever corner of the house you have space — then five swings before you leave for the course.</p>",
        "ggA7TxB0001_text": "<p>Results vary from golfer to golfer. But the first real <i>click</i> at the ball is usually the moment something clicks in your head, too.</p>"
      },
      "type": "gp-section-644851724707693495"
    },
